    "justice": {
      "desktop": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"100%\" style=\"height:auto\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 420\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"nv5-justice-en-desktop-title nv5-justice-en-desktop-desc\" xml:lang=\"en\" data-kind=\"justice\" data-variant=\"desktop\"><title id=\"nv5-justice-en-desktop-title\">Documents, proceedings and judicial oversight</title><desc id=\"nv5-justice-en-desktop-desc\">A general diagram of US federal procedure, not an assignment of procedural status to any particular case. In civil antitrust, a civil investigative demand gathers records; an action or settlement can then go before a court, which rules on remedies. In criminal proceedings, subpoenaed material can be considered by a grand jury; a possible charge leads to proceedings before a judge. Solid links represent evidence gathering; dotted links represent possible subsequent steps. A document demand and a final decision occupy different stages.</desc><rect x=\"1\" y=\"1\" width=\"798\" height=\"418\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--color-surface)\" stroke=\"var(--color-line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"24\" y=\"33\" font-family=\"l0g Nvidia State Leverage\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"var(--color-paper)\" data-zone=\"header\">Documents, proceedings and judicial oversight</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"61\" font-family=\"l0g Nvidia State Leverage\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"var(--color-muted)\" data-zone=\"header\">Federal procedure · general framework</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"101\" font-family=\"l0g Nvidia State Leverage\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"var(--color-signal)\" data-zone=\"channel\">CIVIL ANTITRUST</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"260\" font-family=\"l0g Nvidia State Leverage\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"var(--color-amber)\" data-zone=\"channel\">CRIMINAL</text><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"229\" x2=\"776\" y2=\"229\" stroke=\"var(-